# Figure style sheet. See references/style-guide.md.
#
#     import matplotlib.pyplot as plt
#     plt.style.use("figure.mplstyle")
#
# Every rule here used to be a line of prose telling you to remember it. A style
# sheet is matplotlib's own mechanism for this, so the guide states the intent
# once and this file enforces it.
#
# TRAP: `#` starts a comment in this format, so colors are written BARE -
# `grid.color: e1e0d9`, never `#e1e0d9`. With the hash, matplotlib logs "does
# not look like a color arg" and silently keeps its defaults. Nothing gates
# color on the figure side, so every other check still passes.
#
# TRAP: do not save with `bbox_inches="tight"`. It trims to the drawn content,
# so the saved width is no longer the width you authored - and `page_scale()`
# in check_figure.py derives the type floor from `fig.get_size_inches()`. The
# gate then certifies a size the shipped file does not have. Same species as
# the one above: silent, and every check still passes. `savefig.bbox` below
# pins it. Size the figure with `figsize`; let constrained_layout do the rest.

# --- typeface ---------------------------------------------------------------
# EDIT THIS to match the surrounding document's body face - figures set in the
# document's own voice read as authored, figures in matplotlib's default read as
# pasted in. It is the single largest visual-quality lever here and it costs one
# line. Keep an always-available face last: STIXGeneral and DejaVu ship with
# matplotlib, so the fallback needs nothing installed. Verify the weight you
# need exists - a missing bold resolves silently to regular and flattens the
# whole hierarchy to one weight.
font.family:        serif
font.serif:         STIX Two Text, STIXGeneral, DejaVu Serif
mathtext.fontset:   stix

# --- type sizes -------------------------------------------------------------
# On-page targets divided by the figure's scale. Nothing below 7.5pt on page.
#
# `axes.titlesize`/`titleweight` are for PANEL titles in a multi-panel figure -
# the `(a)` `(b)` register. A single-panel figure carries no internal title:
# the caption names the figure, and a title above the axes just says it twice
# in a second typographic voice.
font.size:          11
axes.titlesize:     13
axes.titleweight:   bold
axes.labelsize:     11
xtick.labelsize:    10
ytick.labelsize:    10
legend.fontsize:    9.5

# --- ink --------------------------------------------------------------------
figure.facecolor:   white
axes.facecolor:     white
text.color:         000000
axes.labelcolor:    52514e
axes.edgecolor:     c3c2b7
xtick.color:        898781
ytick.color:        898781

# --- structure: journal figure, not dashboard -------------------------------
axes.spines.top:    False
axes.spines.right:  False
axes.grid:          True
grid.color:         e1e0d9
grid.linewidth:     0.7
grid.alpha:         1.0
legend.frameon:     False
axes.axisbelow:     True

# --- marks ------------------------------------------------------------------
# Hairlines, not dashboard strokes. These were prose in the guide and passed by
# hand at every call site (`lw=1.6` in the README example), which is the same
# failure mode the rest of this file exists to fix: a rule you have to remember
# is a rule that eventually gets forgotten.
lines.linewidth:        1.6
lines.markersize:       5
lines.markeredgewidth:  0.0
axes.linewidth:         0.8
patch.linewidth:        0.7
legend.handlelength:    1.6
errorbar.capsize:       2.5

# --- output -----------------------------------------------------------------
# The method says render a PNG and look at it. At matplotlib's default 100 dpi
# that PNG is soft enough to hide exactly the type and hairline defects you
# opened it to find. `savefig.bbox: standard` keeps the saved width equal to
# the authored width - see the TRAP note at the top.
figure.dpi:         150
savefig.dpi:        300
# `standard` reads back from rcParams as None - that is matplotlib's own
# spelling for it, not a parse failure. `tight` is the one that reads back as
# a string, and it is the one you do not want.
savefig.bbox:       standard
savefig.facecolor:  white
savefig.transparent: False
